AB  - Accurate wind-direction forecasts are key to yaw control and reduced loss, but standard models struggle with circular wrap-around (0°/360°) and fixed-frame dependence under changing winds. To overcome these challenges, this study presents a forecasting framework that harnesses temporal rotational invariance via deep reinforcement learning, unifying angular classification with continuous regression. A Twin Delayed Deep Deterministic Policy Gradient (TD3) agent adaptively rotates the polar coordinate reference frame by determining the optimal anchor angle for each historical window, eliminating cyclic jumps and aligning the series to the dominant wind direction. The realigned data then undergo a compression-correction step that distils the most informative patterns before being passed to a multi-head attention stacked regressor, which accurately captures short-term fluctuations. The Rotation Alignment and Augmented Representation (RAAR) method was rigorously evaluated through controlled rotation frequency and granularity, interpretability analyses, ablation experiments, and cross-validation on marine datasets from the Gulf of Mexico, Hawaiian waters, Southeast Asia and the Arctic. Under rolling four-window cross validation, RAAR cuts mean absolute error 20–32 % and gains a further 8.5 % with attentive stacking, eliminating cyclic jumps and frame dependence to improve accuracy, robustness, and generalization.
