AB  - This study conducts a comprehensive multi-objective optimization of fin-and-tube heat exchangers utilizing curved rectangular vortex generators (VGs) placed in pairs around the tubes. Fin-and-tube heat exchangers are often constrained by low air-side convective heat transfer coefficients and considerable flow-induced pressure drops. While VGs have shown potential for enhancing performance, the specific impact of grouped winglets around tubes has not been thoroughly explored, highlighting a critical gap addressed in this work. Two distinct winglet placements are analyzed: case A, with both winglets mounted on the same fin surface, and case B, with winglets positioned on opposite fin surfaces. A total of 1080 simulations were conducted, and responses for each design were evaluated using computational fluid dynamics (CFD). The CFD analysis has been carried out by solving mass, momentum, and energy conservation equations using the steady-state k-w SST-turbulence model. The CFD data trained the Artificial neural networks (ANN) which served as a surrogate model for a multi-objective genetic algorithm (GA). The ANN training achieved regression coefficients close to unity, and the optimized Pareto-front points exhibited mean deviations of 1.85 % for the Colburn factor and 0.62 % for the friction factor compared with CFD evaluations, thereby confirming the reliability and predictive accuracy of the optimization process. The optimized designs demonstrated a remarkable performance enhancement of 92.75 % over the baseline geometry and exhibited exceptional performance under off-design conditions. These findings introduce a novel and efficient winglet placement strategy and underscore their potential for broader practical application in compact heat exchanger systems.
