AB  - Context
Soybean leaf diseases pose a significant threat to global agricultural productivity, with primary detection being critical for timely interventions. The methods for disease identification support from limited datasets and generalization, especially in complex fields.
Objective
This study aims to develop Quantum-Spectral Multi-Task Learning Fusion Network (QSpec-MTLFuseNet), a novel multi-task hybrid architecture designed to detect, diagnose soybean leaf diseases, including lesion segmentation, disease classification and severity regression.
Methodology
The proposed model combines quantum token embeddings (QTE), frequency-aware attention via spectral channel transformer blocks (SCTB), and multi-scale feature fusion to capture both spatial and spectral features, demonstrating the application of artificial intelligence (AI) methods, including deep learning. A supervised contrastive pre-training phase is used to enhance class separability. The model is trained using an Auburn soybean disease image dataset (ASDID). Multi-Scale Cross Attention Fusion (MSCAF) is applied to intelligently combine features from various scales during upsampling, improving the model's segmentation and classification performance.
Results
QSpec-MTLFuseNet accomplishes 94.3 % accuracy, 93.8 % precision, and 94.1 % recall in disease classification, with a 0.056 Mean Squared Error (MSE) and 0.91 R2 in severity regression. The model outperforms state-of-the-art techniques, such as Shifted Window Transformer (Swin Transformer) (93.8 %) and Residual Network with 50 layers (ResNet50) (93.7 %), with a mean average precision (mAP) of 83.34 %. The F-beta score (F2 score) of 0.94 highlights the model's focus on high Recall, ensuring disease detection with minimal false negatives.
Future scope
It will focus on expanding the model's application to additional disease categories which detected in the field or smartphone applications.
