AB  - Large language models (LLMs) demonstrate remarkable performances across diverse domains, with DeepSeek-R1 being a representative one. This paper devises a DeepSeek-R1-based code generation framework, automatically achieving the code generation, code correction, model optimization, and result evaluation through iterative interactions between user and LLM. The programming performance of DeepSeek-R1 is comprehensively evaluated in terms of code correctness, code stability, and prediction accuracy, focusing on three promising application paradigms for concrete-filled steel tubular (CFST) structures, including finite element (FE), machine learning (ML), and deep learning (DL) models. Results indicate that DeepSeek-R1 typically generates sophisticated modeling codes with reasonable logicality and high feasibility. Among three application scenarios, DeepSeek-R1 excels in generating modeling codes for ML and DL models, which generally execute correctly even without any corrections. However, DeepSeek-R1 exhibits certain limitations in applying domain-specific knowledge, necessitating user-guided corrections to ensure code accuracy. For high-freedom tasks like algorithm selection and model optimization, generated codes exhibit low stability unless detailed methodological guidance is incorporated into the prompts. Comparatively, the code stability is higher in the FE modeling due to its relatively standardized workflow. Models developed by DeepSeek-R1 consistently achieve highly accurate prediction results across three scenarios, highlighting the potential of LLMs in assisting the design and maintenance of CFST structures. The advantages and limitations are further discussed for the proposed code generation framework, along with potential solutions to address these limitations.
