AB  - Depot location and scheduling schemes play a crucial role in the operation preparation stage in large-scale urban delivery systems. However, traditional two-stage optimization methods that decouple depot location and routing decisions often result in suboptimal solutions and fail to handle heterogeneous service types and time constraints effectively. This study focuses on the multi-depot multi-unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) location-routing problem with time windows under heterogeneous services (MDMULRP-THS), which aims to jointly optimize depot locations, customer assignments, and UAV routing decisions while considering delivery-only, pickup-only, and combined services with soft time windows. The problem is formulated as a complex coupled bi-level optimization model, and a collaborative bi-level optimization approach is proposed to solve it. The upper level determines depot locations and customer assignments using an improved fuzzy clustering algorithm (IFCM) enhanced by an adaptive neighborhood search (ANS) operator. The lower level optimizes UAV routing through a dual-population cooperative multi-objective evolutionary algorithm (DPCMOEA). The two levels interact iteratively through the cost feedback mechanism, which enables the dynamic coordination between location and routing decisions. Experiments on 26 extended benchmark instances demonstrate the effectiveness and superiority of the proposed method over existing approaches. For instance, in instance c130u3w5, the total objective value decreases from 8563.97 to 6795.46, which represents a 20.65 % reduction. Meanwhile, the HV metric increases from 0.815 to 0.841, indicating a 3.19 % improvement in convergence and diversity. Moreover, a real-world instance is used to further validate the practicality of the algorithm, which explores the impact of key parameters and provides managerial insights for the deployment and operation of multi-UAV logistics systems.
