AB  - A brain tumor is unusual tissues present in the brain region that leads to an obstruction for normal activity of brain functionalities. The brain tumor must be detected and diagnosed at an early stage to provide correct treatment and ensure good health. Magnetic Resonance Image (MRI) images are crucial in identifying brain tumors as it provides detailed and high-resolution images of brain structures. This research work implemented an adaptive 3 layered convolutional neural network architecture and eXtreme Gradient Boost method to predict and segment the brain tumor in Magnetic Resonance Image scanned images. An adaptive convolutional neural network 3 layered architecture is developed to analyse and predict the existence of tumor tissues in the brain images. If the prediction is affirmative, then the brain tumor region is segmented using eXtreme Gradient Boost algorithm and morphological functions. The eXtreme Gradient Boost segregates the tumor tissues by increasing the minimal distance between tumor pixels and non-tumor pixels. Finally morphological processes are employed to identify the accurate affected region in brain images. The performance of the developed algorithm is tested on Kaggle dataset and Bangladesh brain Magnetic Resonance Image dataset with considering variety of challenges such as class imbalance, image quality variations, and potential overfitting. Experimental analysis shows that the proposed method provides promising and encouraging results with 99 % accuracy, 99 % sensitivity, 95 % specificity and 98 % dice score. This indicates that the proposed model successfully locates abnormal tissues in brain images.
