AB  - Intelligent diagnostic models require large amounts of training data to achieve excellent diagnostic performance. However, in real-world industrial scenarios, equipment fault data are extremely scarce and prone to noise interference, which significantly degrades the diagnostic capabilities of models. To address this critical challenge, we re-examined the intrinsic characteristics of bearing fault signals from the perspectives of both fault mechanisms and classical signal processing principles for feature extraction. Building on this foundation, we customized a Finite Impulse Response (FIR) filter kernel for the Quadratic Neuron (QN), and proposed an Informed Quadratic Neuron (IQN). This neuron is designed to extract key features of bearing faults by leveraging sampling frequency information through a customized FIR filter. For the primary task, we developed an Informed Quadratic Neuron Residual Neural Network (IQNRNN) to enhance the robustness and generalization capabilities of existing models. In parallel, we innovatively introduced the Gini Index (GI) as a source of prior knowledge for the auxiliary task, and designed a novel Knowledge Informed Loss (KIL) function. Through a dedicated shared network architecture, the domain knowledge acquired from the auxiliary task is effectively transferred to the primary task, thereby reducing the model's reliance on large training datasets. Extensive experiments and ablation studies conducted on public and SUDA datasets demonstrate that our proposed method achieves competitive results even when trained with extremely limited samples.
