AB  - Grey relational analysis (GRA), a powerful tool for pattern recognition and feature selection in data processing, has been widely applied in fields such as energy systems, economics, and environmental management. To address model selection challenges, we propose a data-driven grey adaptive relational analysis (GARA) framework, enhancing traditional panel data GRA models through permutation and combination principles to reduce sensitivity to indicator ordering. First, a GRA model candidate set (GRA-MCS) is constructed, with its consistency coefficient determining selection necessity. Information redundancy in system samples then refines the GRA-MCS into a GRA preliminary candidate set (GRA-PCS), further narrowed to a GRA selected model set (GRA-SMS) via consistency analysis. Stability assessment finally identifies the optimal GRA model. This modeling approach is then applied to energy engineering applications to identify the renewable energy drivers in the Yellow River Basin. The results reveal that value-added of the secondary industry, total population, carbon emissions, urbanization rate, and total energy consumption are key drivers. Furthermore, the results indicate that the GARA modeling approach demonstrates both strong theoretical soundness and practical effectiveness, while comparative analysis confirms the robustness and stability of its modelling framework.
