AB  - Intelligent pest detection is a crucial application of target detection in agriculture since it improves pest monitoring efficiency while ensuring crop quality and productivity. Pest detection is challenging for the complex detection model, the small size and dense distribution of the pests, which lead to poor real-time detection and low accuracy. To address these problems, we propose a fast and accurate pest detection models. First, we replace the feature extraction module in the model with an improved adaptive feature extraction module, which reduces the complexity of the model through adaptive partial convolution improved by the efficient channel attention (ECA) mechanism. Second, to enhance the multi-scale feature extraction capability, we add the spatial pyramid pooling module into a hierarchical structure, which includes three pooling layers at different scales and a global sensory field layer. Finally, we replace the original loss function with the minimum point distance based border similarity comparison metric loss function (MPDIoU), which improves the localization accuracy and model robustness. Experiments on a large-scale multi-target standardized data set of agricultural pests (Pest24) show that our model reduces parameters by 26 % while increasing the mean average precision (mAP) containing 24 pest classes by 0.4 %–75.2 %, outperforming the current state-of-the-art You Only Look Once version 9 (YOLOv9) in terms of accuracy. In addition, experimental results on a homemade dataset containing 19 pest categories show an increase in mAP of 2.8 %–60.5 %. Additionally, we design a pest detection system based on our model for practical pest detection tasks.
