AB  - The unorganized smoke and dust emissions generated from multiple workstations during the steelmaking process pose serious threats to both the environment and the health of operators. To achieve accurate online monitoring and control of smoke emissions, this study proposes an intelligent smoke recognition framework based on Class-Distance-based Feature Selection (CDFS) and classical machine learning algorithms. Smoke image datasets were collected from three typical workstations in steelmaking plants, from which 212 features were extracted. The feature space was then reduced using the CDFS algorithm, and five machine learning models were subsequently trained for classification. Experimental results showed that the model combining CDFS and Random Forest (CDFS-RF) achieved the best overall performance across multiple datasets, with an average Accuracy of 97.59 % and an F1 score of 0.982, outperforming deep learning models such as VGG16 (Visual Geometry Group 16-layer network), ResNet50 (Residual Network 50 layers), YOLOv8 (You Only Look Once version 8) and MobileNetV2. To enhance model interpretability, SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) were employed to analyze the contribution of each feature in the CDFS-RF model. A simplified testing platform was built for real-time testing, and the CDFS-RF model achieved an average computation time of 0.429 s per frame, confirming its applicability in industrial scenarios. This study provides a theoretical foundation for intelligent smoke recognition and control in steelmaking environments.
