AB  - A fundamental challenge in deploying machine learning models in science and engineering is the asymmetry between data-rich training environments and data-scarce inference scenarios. While development often leverages rich, multimodal auxiliary data (e.g., full-field simulation outputs or dense sensor arrays), deployed models must operate using only a limited set of primary inputs. This forces a trade-off: either discard valuable auxiliary information, limiting model fidelity, or build models that cannot be deployed. We resolve this dilemma by introducing a distillation-centric teacher–student framework tailored for physical systems. We propose a teacher architecture with a novel residual fusion mechanism that structures its latent space as a direct augmentation of the primary input’s embedding. This high-fidelity representation of the underlying physics is then transferred to a lightweight student via a representation-level distillation loss. This approach enables the student to inherit the teacher’s complex, multimodal understanding while operating solely on primary data. Validated across benchmarks in fluid dynamics, structural mechanics, and geotechnical engineering, our method reduces prediction error by up to 30% and demonstrates remarkable data efficiency, outperforming even the privileged teacher in data-scarce regimes. Results demonstrate that, by treating auxiliary modalities as privileged information and distilling their latent structure into the student, we can preserve the benefits of multimodal training while requiring only deployment-feasible inputs at inference. This closes the training–inference modality gap and yields compact, robust surrogates that are practical, data-efficient, and ready for reliable use in real-world scientific and engineering settings.
