AB  - Electromagnetic actuation is increasingly important in medical robotics for contactless manipulation and the miniaturization of surgical tools, yet achieving autonomous operation with sufficient feasibility and dexterity remains challenging. In this work, we propose a mixed demonstration framework combining virtual and physical data to enrich training diversity, improve generalization, and bridge the simulation-to-reality gap in electromagnetic actuation. We further introduce a conditional generative policy based on diffusion models for electromagnetic field planning, which bypasses traditional multi-step pipelines by directly generating time-horizon control signals. Our approach supports real-time deployment with diverse interfaces (e.g., human, audio, and large language models) and significantly outperforms regression- and estimation-based baselines across three surgical instruments and working contexts. These results demonstrate robust and dexterous autonomy, highlighted by strong transferability and scalability across real-world scenarios.
