AB  - Micro-electrical connectors are critical components for current and signal transmission, where defects may cause poor contact, signal attenuation, or short circuits, leading to system failures. To enhance stability, defective parts must be efficiently eliminated before assembly, yet existing methods struggle to balance accuracy and real-time performance. We propose a lightweight and robust detection network based on dual-branch hybrid feature extraction and a multi-scale granularity-enhanced encoder (DMNet). Specifically, the dual-branch adaptive feature extraction module employs a state-space model to integrate multi-path contextual information and convolutional projection to capture local defect features, achieving efficient fusion of global long-range dependencies and local details. The edge-aware upsampling module combines cyclic directional displacement with dynamic weight reassembly to improve sensitivity to small defects. The multi-scale dynamic bias granularity-enhanced encoder integrates positional bias, multi-scale convolution, and fine-grained enhancement to strengthen localization of deformed defects. We constructed a multi-class dataset containing five defect categories. Experiments show that DMNet outperforms mainstream models in both accuracy and efficiency, achieving 5.6% higher mean average precision, 24.1% fewer parameters, and 104 frame per second (FPS) detection speed compared with real-time detection transformer (RT-DETR), thereby realizing an optimal balance between accuracy and efficiency.
