AB  - Centrifugal pumps are essential electro-mechanical systems widely used for fluid transport and energy conversion, but mechanical faults in these pumps can introduce irregular, nonstationary characteristics in vibration signals due to complex fluid-structure interactions. Conducting efficient fault diagnosis on these systems is therefore crucial for ensuring operational safety and reducing maintenance costs. However, these signal complexities pose significant challenges for reliable fault detection, as conventional methods often fail to capture transient, fault-related features. To address this, the present study proposes a novel fault diagnosis approach based on newly developed vulnerable grams and deep learning techniques. Wavelet coherence analysis is performed between a healthy baseline and signals from normal and faulty operating conditions to generate coherograms that reveal frequency correlations, which are further enhanced using Savitzky-Golay and spatial moving average filters to produce VGs that effectively highlight fault-induced dynamic anomalies. For automated diagnosis, an end-to-end deep learning framework integrating convolutional neural networks and long short-term memory networks is employed to extract local and spatio-temporal features from the VGs, followed by classification using a shallow multi-layer perceptron. The proposed method is validated on real-world data from a multistage industrial centrifugal pump operating under three different pressure scenarios, achieving high classification accuracies of 99.6 %, 98.2 %, and 97.9 %, and demonstrating superior performance compared to existing techniques, providing a reliable and interpretable framework for real-time centrifugal pump fault diagnosis.
