AB  - Existing large language model based event argument extraction approaches typically concatenate different types of the input information, neglecting their inter-dependencies. This oversight results in the Attention Impairment problem, where models misinterpret or conflate distinct components of the input. To address this, this paper categorizes the input information into four types based on their sources and knowledge levels: event context information, event element information, semantic structure information, and task conversion information. Based on their distinct functional roles, dependencies among these types are analyzed, leading to the proposal of a Layered Attention mechanism designed to mitigate the Attention Impairment problem. In this mechanism, the four information types are sequentially aggregated — starting with basic elements and progressing to the overall task perspective — enabling the model to form a systematic understanding of the input information and avoid misinterpretation. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed method achieves new state-of-the-art performance on two benchmark datasets: ACE05 and WikiEvents. Specifically, it yields gains of 1.7% and 1.9% in argument classification F1 score compared to previous state-of-the-art approaches, respectively.
