AB  - Cross-subject Motor imagery electroencephalogram (MI-EEG) classification is a vital process in building noninvasive brain-computer interfaces (BCIs) due to the distribution shifts faced with lots of subjects. However, existing methods faced from two shortcomings: 1) the complementarity between labels and nonstationary features across subjects; 2) the negative transfer problem caused by accumulated error pseudo-labels. To this end, we propose a novel Multiple Regularized Knowledge Transfer (MRKT) learning method. Our method builds on the maximum mean discrepancy (MMD) minimization framework with Riemannian tangent space features, and adopts four regularizations: (1) high class discriminability; (2) high density samples selection; (3) high confidence parameters during adaptation, and (4) high confidence pseudo-labels selection. The innovation of MRKT is the first one to consider selecting high discriminative and density samples as well as selecting high confidence pseudo-labels during feature adaptation. Extensive experiments and ablation studies on three popular MI-EEG datasets have found that MRKT outperforms recently proposed baselines, and the adopted four regularizations are also very important for feature adaptation. Based on parameter sensitivity experiments, our MRKT method provides a novel calibration option for new subjects in MI-BCIs with high robustness.
