AB  - Lung cancer, often triggered by nodules in the lungs, is one of the deadliest diseases. Early detection greatly improves survival rates, yet spotting malignant nodules in Computed Tomography scans remains complex and time-intensive. Early identification of lung cancer significantly increases the successful treatment outcomes. Hence, this paper presents an effective model named Shuffle Deep Neural Network to detect lung cancer. Here, the Shuffle Deep Neural Network is established by the amalgamation of ShuffleNet along with the Deep Neural Network. At first, the input lung image is given for the pre-processing process, which is accomplished by utilizing a Gaussian filter to remove the unwanted noise. After that, the lung lobe segmentation is accomplished based on Dual-Attention V-Network and nodule segmentation is done by Improved V-Net. Furthermore, the feature extraction for the segmented image is conducted, and the extracted features are Local Ternary Pattern, statistical-based Local Vector Pattern, as well as Weber Local Descriptor. Ultimately, the detection of lung cancer is performed using the proposed Shuffle Deep Neural Network model. Additionally, a developed Shuffle Deep Neural Network attained better specificity, accuracy, and sensitivity of 0.937, 0.918, and 0.958 while considering the K-value as 8.
