KW  - Fabric defect detection
KW  - Intelligent textiles
KW  - Target recognition
KW  - Deep learning
KW  - Attention mechanism
AB  - Mechanical deviations during the weaving process constitute a primary cause of fabric defects, with variations in nozzle angle, sustained error duration, and applied tension leading to significant morphological, dimensional, and spatial heterogeneity—even among defects of the same type. Current fabric defect detection methods face limitations in inference speed, localization accuracy, and detection reliability. To address these challenges, this study proposes an enhanced defect detection model (Neekolo) based on You Only Look Once Version 8(YOLOv8). The proposed framework introduces three key innovations: A supplementary detection layer is integrated into the feature extraction network to optimize the model's architecture. Coupled with ghost convolution to minimize parameter redundancy and feature overlap; Leveraging morphological and distributional characteristics of fabric defects, the feature fusion network combines Omni-dimensional Dynamic Convolution (ODConv) and Deformable Large Kernel Attention (DLKA) to enhance dynamic shape adaptation and spatial localization; A novel Neeko attention mechanism is designed to jointly optimize global-local spatial and channel information. Experimental validation was conducted on both a custom-labeled dataset (few-category defects dataset) and a public multi-category defect dataset. Compared to the baseline YOLOv8, the proposed model achieves 1.9 % and 10.5 % improvements in Mean Average Precision by 50 % (mAP50) on the respective datasets while significantly accelerating inference speed. These results demonstrate the model's robustness across defect variability and its potential for industrial deployment.
