KW  - Energy trading
KW  - Smart grid
KW  - Smart grid stability
KW  - Machine learning
AB  - The rapid evolution of smart grids, driven by the increasing integration of renewable energy sources and decentralized energy generation, presents critical challenges for secure and stable energy management. To address these challenges, this study explores the integration of Blockchain technology and investigates Machine Learning (ML) models to enhance the security and stability of energy transactions within smart grids. Blockchain is integrated to enable secure, transparent, and tamper-proof peer-to-peer (P2P) energy trading, while ML techniques are employed to predict grid stability under varying operational conditions. For empirical analysis, the publicly available University of California Irvine (UCI) Electrical Grid Stability Simulated Dataset is utilized, comprising 10,000 instances with features representing grid operational parameters. We implement and compare several ML algorithms, including Logistic Regression (LR), Support Vector Machine (SVM), Decision Tree (DT), Random Forest (RF), K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN), Adaptive Boosting (AdaBoost), eXtreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost), and Artificial Neural Networks (ANN). Experimental results indicate that the XGBoost classifier achieves superior performance, with 99.04 % accuracy, an Area Under the Curve (AUC) of 1.00, and recall and F1-scores of 0.99. These findings demonstrate the potential of integrating Blockchain for transaction security and traditional ML models for grid stability prediction, rather than deep learning models, paving the way for smarter, more resilient energy infrastructures.
